        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          Not quite ready for full electric? Learn the difference between self-charging hybrids and plug-in hybrids (PHEVs).
        </p>

        <hr class="my-8 border-slate-100">

        <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Self-charging hybrid vs. plug-in hybrid (PHEV)</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Full / Self-Charging Hybrids</strong> combine a petrol engine with an electric motor and a small battery that tops itself up through braking and engine power. You never need to plug them in, and they shine in stop-start town driving.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plug-In Hybrids (PHEVs)</strong> have a larger battery you can charge from a wallbox or public charger. That lets you cover shorter daily trips on electric power alone, while still having a petrol engine for longer motorway journeys.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">How to tell if a PHEV suits you</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Charging habits:</strong> PHEVs deliver their best running costs when charged regularly—ideally at home or work.</li>
            <li><strong>Trip lengths:</strong> If your regular commute fits within the car's electric-only range, you can cut fuel stops noticeably.</li>
            <li><strong>Peace of mind on long trips:</strong> You get flexibility for long journeys without planning every stop around a rapid charger.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

